import { PracticalCollectionCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> PracticalCollectionCopy = PracticalCollectionCopy {
  summary: "items.drop skips the requested number of leading List elements and returns the remainder. A count of zero or less keeps everything; at or beyond the length, the result is empty.",
  taskTitle: "Skip displayed items to reach the next page",
  purpose: "Continue after an item that has already been displayed. Skip Read, then take at most two items to create a page containing Review and Ship. Also check negative, zero, and at-or-beyond-length skip counts.",
  typescriptBefore: "For a normal array, slice(1, 3) selects two items after skipping the first: include position 1 and stop before position 3. The boundary checks use slice(Math.max(0, count)) to treat negative counts as zero. slice(-1) returns only the last item, unlike drop’s negative-count policy. The array compares this task; it does not have List’s representation.",
  benefit: "drop takes the number to skip, and take takes the maximum number to display. Together they express a page using counts. TypeScript’s slice can also do this while leaving the original data unchanged.",
  setup: "items is the alias for std/list. The backtick before [ in `[\"Read\", \"Review\", \"Ship\"] creates a List. Brackets without it create the separate Array type. items.drop 1 labels passes the Int count first and the List second.",
  after: [
    "The first expression evaluates items.drop 1 labels inside the parentheses to skip Read. Passing that result to items.take 2 displays [\"Review\",\"Ship\"]. On its own, drop returns every item remaining after the skipped count.",
    "describe accepts List<String>. The comprehension [value | value <- values] reads each List element and makes an Array for JSON display. Its opening bracket has no backtick. The result of drop itself is still a List.",
    "After the four take boundary calls, the four drop calls use counts -1, 0, 3, and 4. -1 and 0 retain all three elements; 3 and 4 produce []. (0 - 1) produces the integer -1. Both following empty-input calls give [], and the last line displays all three original labels unchanged.",
  ],
  details: [
    "Negative and zero counts skip nothing. Skipping at least the full length returns an empty List. Empty input stays empty too; reaching the end does not return Nothing or throw an exception.",
    "The count must be Int, so a Float such as 1.5 does not fit. 0, False, and an empty string each count as an element to skip. Unlike filter, drop uses only the number of leading elements rather than a condition on their values.",
    "List drop walks past the requested number of elements and returns the remaining tail. That tail can be shared with the original List, so this does not mean copying every remaining element. Sharing does not mean drop removes from or rewrites the original List.",
    "There is no need to calculate length first: drop returns an empty List when it reaches the end. Combine the remainder with head if only its first item is needed, or with take to limit the page size.",
  ],
}
